    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-biophysics-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Biophysics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Biophysics - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Assistant Professor, Biophysics Assistant Professor, Biophysics 499892 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Sep 29 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Position overview: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at Boise State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the field of experimental biophysics. This position starts in August 2027. We are seeking candidates who share our commitment to research and teaching. The new hire will be expected to develop a vigorous, externally funded research program that supports PhD graduate student training and engages undergraduate students. The new hire will teach existing or new courses, as needed. The Department of Physics and Astronomy offers a flexible workload policy that allows a balance of research, teaching, and service that is appropriate for career stage and academic focus. In their first year, faculty with substantial research expectations typically receive a reduced teaching and service workload.  Faculty who maintain high levels of research activity and multiple PhD students generally receive a reduced workload. This position is part of Boise State University's interdisciplinary Biomolecular Sciences (BMOL) Cluster Hire Initiative, with open positions in the departments of Biological Sciences , Chemistry & Biochemistry , Mathematics , and Physics and Astronomy . The goal is to support a cohort of scientists with the potential to create new interdisciplinary research collaborations, support graduate education and the growth of doctoral programs, position Boise State for long-term research distinction, and build on existing institutional strengths. We strongly encourage applications from candidates whose research has biomedical applications and strong potential to attract NIH and other (NSF, etc.) externally sponsored research funding. Candidates should describe the potential for their research to contribute to at least one of the existing or emerging areas of interdisciplinary strength at Boise State University, including Matrix Biology COBRE , the Convergent Engineering and Biomolecular Science (CEBS) COBRE , Healthy Idaho Clinical and Translational Research (CTR-D) Program , Food and Dairy Innovation Center , Neuroscience , Cancer-related research, or Quantum Information Science applied to biophysics. Applicants should also discuss how their research could leverage Boise State's shared instrumentation, core facilities ( BRCF and FaCT Core ), and collaborative research infrastructure, including resources available through the Biomedical Research Institute , while contributing to a collaborative environment that advances scientific discovery, graduate education, and externally funded research. Department overview: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at Boise State University has a strong commitment to research, teaching, and service. Our faculty members supervise PhD students in Boise State’s interdisciplinary Biomolecular Sciences (BMOL) graduate program and take pride in mentoring students across degree levels. Our department is committed to the success of our faculty and provides both informal and formal mentoring to support career development. Research in our department is focused on biophysics, nanoscience, and astrophysics, creating numerous opportunities for collaboration and growth. In addition, our faculty collaborate across a range of disciplines with faculty at the University and at other institutions. The department offers a flexible workload policy that allows a balance of research and teaching appropriate for career stage and interests. Faculty with high levels of research activity typically teach a reduced workload, and especially in the early years, faculty are offered reduced teaching and service responsibilities. Faculty have access to a state-of-the-art rodent vivarium and zebrafish facility, the Biomolecular Research Core (BRC), shared department spaces, and additional core facilities across campus, such as the Center for Materials Characterization (CMC) . Two distinct NIH-funded Centers of Biomedical Research Excellence (COBRE), one in Matrix Biology and one in Convergent Engineering and Biomolecular Sciences (CEBS), are located within the Biomedical Research Institute. Research core facilities have full-time dedicated staff to maintain instrumentation, provide training, and perform services. About Boise State: Boise State University is a powerhouse of creativity, research, and impact. Our campus sits along the Boise River and reaches into the heart of Idaho’s capital – a vibrant hub for innovation, culture, and outdoor adventure. We’re nationally recognized for trailblazing research, student success, and community impact. Our faculty are scholars, mentors, and changemakers who advance ideas, discoveries, and the next generation of leaders. Boise offers the perfect backdrop – a welcoming city with a thriving arts scene, growing tech sector, and mountains on the horizon. Join us and help shape a bold, collaborative academic community. Learn more about faculty careers at Boise State and the City of Boise . Minimum qualifications: A PhD in physics or a closely related discipline A record of research with biomedical applications Evidence of research accomplishment appropriate to the candidate’s career stage, such as peer-reviewed publications, extramural funding or grant activity, and research mentoring A research agenda with potential to attract funding from the National Institutes of Health, National Science Foundation, or other extramural sources Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, effective teaching and mentoring at the undergraduate and graduate levels Preferred qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in physics Postdoctoral or subsequent research experience in experimental biophysics Experience developing or supporting learning and research environments in which students with varied backgrounds, preparation, and experiences can succeed Research that aligns with one or more of the interdisciplinary strengths described in the position overview Interest in using or contributing to Boise State’s core facilities Required application materials: Cover letter describing your interest in the position and the contributions you would make to the Department of Physics and Astronomy at Boise State University, and to the interdisciplinary Biomolecular Sciences graduate program. Curriculum vitae that includes employment history with dates, publications, grant activity or grant-writing experience, courses taught, student mentoring, leadership roles and responsibilities, service, and other relevant professional activities. Research statement, two pages maximum, describing: Your research accomplishments and experience with extramural funding; Your planned research agenda and its potential for NIH, NSF, and/or other extramural funding; How your work aligns with one or more of the interdisciplinary research strengths described in the position overview; How you anticipate using or contributing to Boise State’s core facilities; and Your experience with or approach to mentoring undergraduate students, graduate students, or postdoctoral researchers. Teaching statement, two pages maximum, describing: Your teaching experience and approach to effective undergraduate and graduate instruction; How you assess student learning, respond to barriers, and improve your teaching; and How you create learning environments in which students with varied backgrounds, preparation, and experiences can succeed. Three representative publications demonstrating your research accomplishments and potential in experimental biophysics. Please note that the application system accepts up to five uploaded documents, with a maximum file size of 10 MB each. Please plan accordingly to ensure all required materials are included. Combine the three representative publications into a single PDF when possible. If they cannot be combined within the file-size limit, upload a single document containing links to the publications. Please also provide the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references. Reference information will be entered directly into the application system and does not need to be uploaded as a separate document. Closing Date: Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026. Applications received by that date will receive full consideration. The position will remain open until filled. For questions regarding this position, please contact physics@boisestate.edu. Salary and Benefits: Starting salary: The 9-month academic year salary ranges from $75,000 to $80,000. Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract (unless otherwise noted) with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract, unless noted that the position is temporary. More information about faculty contracts and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a best-in-class benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents See our full benefits page for more information Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 4d9102ff Last Name (required) bf65736b Email (required) bf49a572 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Associate Director, Ticket Operations Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Athletics, Management, Mid-Level Assistant Teaching Professor of Jazz Studies Boise, Idaho College of Arts & Sciences Assistant Professor, Public Policy and Administration Boise, Idaho College of Education and Public Service Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 30861690 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories College of Arts & Sciences College of Arts & Sciences Employment Type Faculty Faculty Locations Boise, Idaho, United States Boise, Idaho, United States First Name (required) 9ef54f68 Last Name (required) 39a804c5 Email (required) 96b1a621 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Boise State University (208) 426-1000 1910 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83725 Facebook Instagram X Youtube LinkedIn Cookie Preferences Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy © 2026 All Rights Reserved. Boise State University Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
