    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-chemistry-and-biochemistry-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Biochemistry",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Biochemistry - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Biochemistry Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Biochemistry 499874 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Sep 23 2026 Closing on: Oct 25 2026 Add to favorites View favorites The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at Boise State University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date in the Fall semester of 2027. We seek candidates who develop or apply experimental, computational, or data-driven approaches to study chemical or biochemical processes relevant to human health, with preference for research that complements existing strengths at Boise State in either dairy chemistry or biomedical research, or a combination of the two. The successful candidate will be expected to develop and sustain an externally funded research program, mentor undergraduate and graduate researchers, and disseminate research through peer-reviewed publications and other appropriate scholarly venues. This position is part of Boise State University's interdisciplinary Biomolecular Sciences Cluster Hire Initiative, with open positions in the departments of Biological Sciences, Chemistry & Biochemistry, Mathematics, and Physics. The goal is to support a cohort of scientists with the potential to create new interdisciplinary research collaborations, support graduate education and the growth of doctoral programs, position Boise State for long-term research distinction, and build on existing institutional strengths. Biomedical Research - We strongly encourage applications from candidates whose research has biomedical applications and strong potential to attract NIH and other externally sponsored research funding. Candidates should describe the potential for their research to contribute to at least one of the existing or emerging areas of interdisciplinary strength at Boise State University, including Matrix Biology COBRE , the Convergent Engineering and Biomolecular Science (CEBS) COBRE , Healthy Idaho Clinical and Translational Research (CTR-D) Program , Food and Dairy Innovation Center , Neuroscience , Cancer Research, and Quantum Information Science. Applicants should also discuss how their research could leverage Boise State's shared instrumentation, core facilities ( BRCF and FaCT Cores), and collaborative research infrastructure, including resources available through the Biomedical Research Institute , while contributing to a collaborative environment that advances scientific discovery, graduate education, and externally funded research. Dairy Chemistry - Dairy chemistry candidates should have expertise in subject matter related to analytical, physical, computational, biochemical, biomedical, environmental, or process engineering. The successful candidate will be expected to actively engage with the Building University-Industry Linkages through Learning and Discovery (BUILD) Dairy network, support from Dairy Management Inc., involved with public-private partnership and the Western Dairy Center or one of the other regional dairy centers across the United States.  The Food and Dairy Innovation Center at Boise State University offers access to a collaborative state-of-the-art facility that may be leveraged as a resource for the successful dairy chemist. Research-active faculty in the department typically teach an average of one course per semester, with teaching responsibilities including both undergraduate and graduate courses. The successful candidate will be expected to teach existing courses and contribute to the development of new undergraduate electives that complement and expand the Department’s current curriculum, in areas such as food chemistry, environmental chemistry, forensic chemistry, or other topics aligned with their expertise. The successful candidate will also contribute to departmental, university, and professional service. A Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related field is required, as is a demonstrated commitment to excellence in research, effective teaching, and creating an inclusive mentoring environment. Postdoctoral research experience is preferred but not required. We offer dedicated laboratory space, competitive start-up funds and direct access to shared instrumentation and core facilities. Institutional assistantships across interdisciplinary Ph.D. programs are available to support mentored Ph.D. students. Department overview: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry fosters a collaborative and collegial environment that values excellence in teaching, continuous improvement, and a shared commitment to student success. Our vision is to “Promote student success through excellence in chemistry.” We advance this vision through a rigorous curriculum and exceptional research experiences that develop critical thinking, scientific independence, proficiency with modern instrumentation and laboratory practices, and effective scientific communication. Boise State University is a Carnegie-classified R2 institution with high research activity, and the Department is committed to expanding the impact and visibility of its research programs. Faculty conduct research across a broad range of areas, including dairy science, food chemistry, drug discovery and medicinal chemistry, medical devices, cosmochemistry, computational chemistry, enzymology, protein chemistry, and spectroscopy. The Department seeks to further strengthen its research portfolio through this hire in areas that create opportunities for collaboration across biomolecular science, food and dairy chemistry, chemical sensing, and related fields. The Department offers Bachelor of Science and Master of Science degrees in Chemistry. Faculty also mentor Ph.D. students through interdisciplinary doctoral programs, including Biomolecular Sciences, Materials Science and Engineering, Computing, and Biomedical Engineering. These programs provide opportunities for collaboration and graduate mentorship that extend across departmental and disciplinary boundaries. The Department currently comprises 11 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 5 full-time teaching faculty, 8 full-time laboratory instructors, and 10 staff members. We serve approximately 160 undergraduate chemistry majors and mentor 12 M.S. students in Chemistry and 16 Ph.D. students across three interdisciplinary doctoral programs. We are proud of our collaborative culture and our shared commitment to “work in a department that embraces a team spirit of cooperation and respect for all.” About Boise State: Boise State University is a powerhouse of creativity, research, and impact. Our campus sits along the Boise River and reaches into the heart of Idaho’s capital – a vibrant hub for innovation, culture, and outdoor adventure. We’re nationally recognized for trailblazing research, student success, and community impact. Our faculty are scholars, mentors, and changemakers who advance ideas, discoveries, and the next generation of leaders. Boise offers the perfect backdrop – a welcoming city with a thriving arts scene, growing tech sector, and mountains on the horizon. Join us and help shape a bold, collaborative academic community. Learn more about faculty careers at Boise State and the City of Boise . Minimum qualifications: A Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry, Biomedical, Food Science with focus in Dairy Chemistry, or a closely related field Preferred qualifications: Postdoctoral Research Demonstrated commitment to excellence in research Experience using evidence based instructional practices (EBIPS) Demonstrated experience in, or strong potential for, applying for and securing external research funding. Research to contribute to at least one of the existing or emerging areas of interdisciplinary strength at Boise State University as outlined above. Required application materials: Cover letter (1-2 page) Curriculum Vitae Teaching philosophy statement (1 page) Research statement (3–5 pages including a 3-year research project plan, student graduate student engagement, laboratory infrastructure needs, and budget table to detail startup fund investment) Contact information for three professional references Closing Date: Position will close for new applicants on October 25, 2026. For questions regarding this position, please contact the search committee chair, Prof. Owen McDougal by email at owenmcdougal@boisestate.edu. Salary and Benefits: The 9-month academic year salary ranges from $75,000 to $80,000. Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract (unless otherwise noted) with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract, unless noted that the position is temporary. More information about faculty contract and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a best-in-class benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents Dual Career Assistance Support for spouses and partners. See our full benefits page for more information Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 17c405d9 Last Name (required) 2f60cf01 Email (required) d17959ef Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Associate Director, Ticket Operations Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Athletics, Management, Mid-Level Assistant Teaching Professor of Jazz Studies Boise, Idaho College of Arts & Sciences Assistant Professor, Public Policy and Administration Boise, Idaho College of Education and Public Service Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 585ba894 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the 